import{t as e}from"./utils-COoNfFoj.js";var t={slug:`electrified`,influencerName:`Electrified`,influencerHandle:`@DillonLoomis22`,influencerAvatarUrl:`${e}/electrified-avatar.jpg`,heroDescription:`I have been vocal about not being a fan of the current 3rd party tracker tool that almost all of the Tesla community uses. I wanted to create something using more official data that I can continue to update over time and that works seamlessly on desktop and mobile. This is the most important storyline at Tesla right now so I wanted a visually appealing, interactive way for my audience to stay updated on the most relevant metrics.`,assetTitle:`TESLAUTONOMY`,screenshotUrl:`${e}/electrified.png`,remixUrl:`https://electrified.pplx.app/#/`,promptTitle:`Try Electrified's prompt`,promptText:`I want to build a Tesla Robotaxi Command Center: a single-page, four-zone dashboard that tracks the Robotaxi rollout in real time and gives my audience something they can’t get anywhere else. The experience should feel like a Bloomberg Terminal–style dashboard with strong visual density, live data, and tasteful integration of connectors and infographics. The layout should follow a Bloomberg-style grid with four primary zones. Z1, “The Fleet,” should feature a U.S.-only map displaying active Robotaxi cities such as Austin, the San Francisco Bay Area, Dallas, and Houston, along with unsupervised vehicle counts by metro area and a hiring watchlist showing where Tesla is staffing up next. Z2, “The Pipeline,” should track announced cities, “spotted testing” sightings, and rollout signals pulled from job postings. Z3, “The Numbers,” should provide a head-to-head comparison between Tesla and Waymo, including fleet size, cumulative paid miles, geofence square mileage, and safety incidents, while also displaying live TSLA and NDX stock tickers in the header. Z4, “The Timeline,” should display milestone tracking in days only (no hours or minutes), along with recent safety filings, NHTSA recall numbers with direct source links, and key rollout milestones. The primary source of truth for the dashboard should be a live Google Sheet that I maintain, connected through the Google Sheets connector so updates propagate to the site within approximately 60 seconds. A YouTube connector should automatically pull my two latest public videos into a footer content strip. The app should also display live stock prices for TSLA and NDX in the header. Additionally, schedule a daily cron job that snapshots the dashboard, compares it against the previous day’s state, and notifies me whenever a material change is detected. The visual direction should feel clean, light, and distinctly Tesla-inspired, with white surfaces, Gotham-style typography, Tesla branding in the header, thin borders, and restrained accent colors. The interface should be information-dense while remaining highly legible at a glance, avoiding generic dashboard clichés, heavy gradients, or overdesigned AI-style visuals. Mobile responsiveness is critical: the dashboard should scroll cleanly on smaller screens, and the map should support pinch-to-zoom interactions on touch devices.`};export{t as default};
